This is a single-line inline code pair: `{code}ai/gh-125{code}`. With my planned one-line handling: opening `{code}` + rest-of-line `ai/gh-125{code}` — my line-scan treats `{code}` at line start, rest = `ai/gh-125{code}`. First word = `ai/gh-125{code}`?? That's wrong — the whole line would become a code block with lang `ai/gh-125{code}`.

Hmm! The `{code}x{code}` one-line form needs PAIR handling, not "rest of line = language". So the scanner must first check for a one-line PAIR: line matches `^\{code(?::lang)?\}(.*)\{code\}\s*$` → single-line block → '```' + (lang)+ '\n' + inner + '\n```'. The inner = group(1). But careful with greedy matching: `{code}a{code} mid {code}b{code}` — two pairs one line. Use replaceAllMapped for the pair regex on the line: `\{code(?::([\w#+.-]+))?\}([^\n]*?)\{code\}` → '```$1\n$2\n```'. Then, if any pairs were replaced, output result (could be multi-line via embedded newlines ✓).

Then the BLOCK opening check: `^\{code(?::([\w#+.-]+))?\}(.*)$` where the rest does NOT contain `{code}` (pair already handled). lang = explicit or first word of rest; if rest has more words → that's an unclosed block whose first line contains trailing text? E.g. `{code}dart final x` — treat lang=first word, remainder = content of the block's first line. Reasonable.

Order of checks in the scanner (not inCode):
1. If line contains a PAIR (`_inlineCodePair.hasMatch(line)`): replace all pairs in the line; but ALSO the line might have leading text: `Branch: {code}x{code}` — the pair replacement is inline: 'Branch: ```\nx\n```'. For the gh-122 evidence, `*Branch:* {code}ai/gh-125{code}` → `**Branch:** ```\nai/gh-125\n``` — renders as bold label then fenced block ✓ readable.
   BUT: should other transforms (bold) also apply to the non-pair parts of the line? `*Branch:*` needs bold conversion. So: process pairs first, then run remaining transforms on the RESULT? The result now contains ``` fences and the code content — running link/bold transforms over it could corrupt code content (`*not bold*` inside the inline pair!). Hmm.

